<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Faraday</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Faraday was an English scientist who discovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electromagnetic-induction">electromagnetic induction</a> in 1831, the principle behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">electric generators</a> and transformers. Largely self-educated, he worked at the Royal Institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he also made major discoveries in electrochemistry, built an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motor</a> and gave popular lectures on science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-faraday.jpg" alt="Michael Faraday" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Faraday_sitting_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael Faraday was born on 22 September 1791 in Newington Butts, Surrey, now part of the London Borough of Southwark, the third of four children. His family was not well off, and his father, James, was a member of the Glasite sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. He had only the most basic schooling and had to educate himself.</p><p>At 14 he became an apprentice to George Riebau, a bookbinder and bookseller, and during the seven years read widely, including Jane Marcet's Conversations on Chemistry. In 1812 he heard lectures by Humphry Davy at the Royal Institution and sent Davy a 300-page book based on his notes. When Davy damaged his eyesight in an accident with nitrogen trichloride, he hired Faraday, who was appointed Chemical Assistant on 1 March 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Faraday was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1824 and in 1825 became Director of the Laboratory of the Royal Institution. In 1833 he became its first Fullerian Professor of Chemistry, for life and without the obligation to lecture. He founded the Friday Evening Discourses and was the main populariser of the Christmas Lecture Series. He also did public work: with Charles Lyell he wrote a report on a colliery explosion at Haswell in 1846 that killed 95 miners, which indicated for the first time that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> dust worsened explosions. He refused to advise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, declined a knighthood on religious grounds, and twice refused the presidency of the Royal Society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In chemistry Faraday discovered benzene and tetrachloroethylene, liquefied several gases and found the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electrolysis">electrolysis</a>. He popularised the terms anode, cathode, electrode and ion, which William Whewell largely proposed. In 1821 he built two devices producing what he called electromagnetic rotation, one of them the homopolar motor, a foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motor</a> technology.</p><p>In 1845 he discovered diamagnetism and found that a magnetic field can rotate the plane of polarised light, now called the Faraday effect. His ice pail experiment showed that charge sits on the outside of a conductor, the principle of the Faraday cage. His mathematics went no further than simple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell">James Clerk Maxwell</a>, who took up his work, wrote that Faraday's lines of force showed him to have been a mathematician of a very high order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Faraday” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Science and thought flourished: Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859), the work of <b>Michael Faraday</b> and James Clerk Maxwell on electromagnetism, and the economics of David Ricardo and John Stuart Mill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He questioned whether muscular action could explain such movements, but <b>Michael Faraday</b>’s experiments, published in 1853, showed that table-turning was caused by the ideomotor effect.</blockquote><a href="/religion/spiritism">Spiritism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Michael Faraday</b> first demonstrated electromagnetic induction on 29 August 1831, and Joseph Henry discovered it independently in 1832.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Newington Butts, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Hampton Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.1&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Newington Butts on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4548 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michael Faraday"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4998 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4998 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.1&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no better, there is no more open door by which you can enter into the study of natural philosophy, than by considering the physical phenomena of a candle.&#8221;</p><footer>The Chemical History of a Candle (1861), lecture 1 &#183; original &#183; Christmas lectures for young people at the Royal Institution &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14474/pg14474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that you may, like it, shine as lights to those about you; that, in all your actions, you may justify the beauty of the taper by making your deeds honourable and effectual in the discharge of your duty to your fellow-men.&#8221;</p><footer>The Chemical History of a Candle (1861), closing words &#183; original &#183; Christmas lectures for young people at the Royal Institution &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14474/pg14474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
